{#
    The mark in the bar.

    What it says is configuration, not a template a project has to copy. In its
    `guides.xml`:

        <extension class="TYPO3\Soul\GuidesTheme\DependencyInjection\SoulExtension">
            <signet>_images/signet.svg</signet>
            <brand>Acme</brand>
            <product>Your product</product>
        </extension>

    All three are optional. With no product the bar carries the project title
    from `<project>`, which is where a name belongs when there is only one.

    The pipe is drawn by the stylesheet — a 2px accent rule, and one of exactly
    three places that colour appears — so it is written as an empty span and
    never as a `|`. It appears only where there are two halves to separate: a
    single name is the mark itself, in the mark's own weight, rather than the
    quiet half of a lockup with nothing beside it.

    The version is not in here. It is a badge at the other end of the bar, with
    the rest of what is true of the page rather than of the product, and it goes
    with them when the bar narrows.

    The signet path is relative to the documentation root and must be a file
    the renderer can see — beside the documents, so it is copied into the
    output with them rather than pointing at something that exists only on the
    machine that built the site.

    A drawing is referenced and everything else is linked, which is the same
    distinction `sds-image` makes and made here for the same reason: an SVG
    referenced into the page carries the page's ink and the page's accent in
    both modes, while an `<img>` renders its file in a document of its own
    where none of the tokens are declared — so a linked mark keeps whichever
    grey its author baked in on a page that has since gone dark. A raster
    signet is linked, because there is nothing in one for a mode to change.

    The price of being referenced is one line in the file: the root `<svg>`
    names itself `id="art"`, and its colours are written as a `var()` with a
    hex fallback. `docs/guidelines/artwork.rst` is that written out, and an
    SVG signet that does not follow it draws nothing rather than drawing
    wrong.

    No element here. Every other component in this theme is written by the
    renderer and upgraded in the browser, and this is markup with nothing to
    upgrade into: the mark in the bar is the site's identity, and a reader with
    scripting off is a reader who should still see it.

    A signet is NOT an icon: it ships as three drawings, each made in the box
    it is for, and 24 is the one this bar is the size of. A project that
    configures the 16 or the 32 file here gets a drawing between its whole
    multiples, which is the one thing this construction is built to avoid.
#}
<a class="sds-lockup" href="{{ soul.home ? asset(soul.home) : renderLink('/index') }}">
    {%- if soul.signet %}
        {%- if soul.signet ends with '.svg' %}
        <svg class="sds-signet" width="24" height="24" aria-hidden="true"><use href="{{ asset('/' ~ soul.signet) }}#art"></use></svg>
        {%- else %}
        <img class="sds-signet" src="{{ asset('/' ~ soul.signet) }}" alt="" width="24" height="24" />
        {%- endif %}
    {%- endif %}
    {%- set name = soul.product|default(env.projectNode.title)|default('Documentation') %}
    <span class="sds-wordmark">
        {%- if soul.brand -%}
            {{ soul.brand }}<span class="sds-wordmark__pipe" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <span class="sds-wordmark__product">{{ name }}</span>
        {%- else -%}
            {{ name }}
        {%- endif -%}
    </span>
</a>
